; pi4V3dTfu.pi4 - stage 1 of the V3D bring-up. Does the GPU EXECUTE?
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
; WHAT THIS PROGRAM PROVES, AND WHAT IT REFUSES TO CLAIM.
;
;   pi4V3dIdent answers "is the block there". A register that reads
;   $2124 proves the address derivation, the power domain, the clock,
;   the reset and both bridges - and it proves nothing whatever about
;   the engine DOING anything. Reading an ID register is something a
;   dead block with a live bus interface does perfectly well.
;
;   This program hands the Texture Formatting Unit a 256-byte image in
;   DRAM, asks it to rewrite that image into the GPU's tiled layout at
;   a different address, and then CHECKS EVERY OUTPUT BYTE ON THE CPU
;   against a layout computed here from first principles.
;
;   IT DOES NOT BELIEVE A STATUS BIT. TFU_CS's conversion counter
;   moving is necessary and is nowhere near sufficient: a unit that
;   incremented a counter and wrote nothing would look identical from
;   the register side, and "the status said done and the buffer was
;   untouched" is the exact shape of failure this project keeps
;   finding. The pass criterion is 256 bytes in the right places, per
;   job, three times.
;
; WHY THE TFU AND NOT THE GRAPHICS PIPELINE.
;
;   Because it is the smallest thing on this part that executes. It
;   takes eleven register writes. It needs no control list, no binner,
;   no tile state buffer, no tile allocation pool, no shader and no
;   QPU assembler. The bin/render path needs all six of those and the
;   minimum contents of its two command lists are not written down
;   anywhere - v3d.pi4's WHY THERE IS STILL NO CONTROL LIST section
;   works that through and it has not changed.
;
;   The CSD compute dispatcher is the other seven-write candidate and
;   it is not here for one reason: its config registers point at a QPU
;   PROGRAM, so it costs a QPU assembler before it costs anything
;   else.
;
; THE THREE JOBS, AND WHY THERE ARE THREE.
;
;   All three convert a RASTER (plain linear) input into a LINEARTILE
;   output, 32 bits per texel, so a microtile is 4x4 texels and 64
;   bytes. All three move exactly 256 bytes. They differ in the ONE
;   variable each is there to isolate.
;
;   J1  4 wide by 16 high.  THE OUTPUT MUST EQUAL THE INPUT, BYTE FOR
;       BYTE. For an image only one microtile wide, the LINEARTILE
;       address of a texel and its raster address are the same
;       arithmetic - see THE LAYOUT below - so this job needs no
;       tiling model at all to check. If J1 passes, bytes moved from
;       one address to another because the GPU moved them. That is
;       the claim in the title of this program and J1 alone settles
;       it.
;
;   J2  16 wide by 4 high.  THE OUTPUT IS A PERMUTATION OF THE INPUT
;       and the permutation is not the identity. Four microtiles side
;       by side; texel (5,2) sits at raster offset $94 and at
;       LINEARTILE offset $64. This job needs the layout model, and
;       that is the point: J1 says the engine moved bytes, J2 says the
;       engine FORMATTED them. If J1 passes and J2 fails, the fault is
;       in the layout model or in the tiling encoding and not in the
;       bring-up, which is a much smaller thing to go and look at.
;
;   J3  16 wide by 4 high again, and the ONLY difference from J2 is
;       the texture format code: RGBA8 instead of R32F. Both are four
;       bytes per texel and the TFU does no format conversion, so the
;       output must be byte-identical to J2's. This is not padding:
;       Mesa deliberately rewrites the format to a generic one of the
;       same width for an exact copy (mesa-24.3.4_v3dx_tfu.c:66-77)
;       and never says why it has to. "Should behave identically" is
;       an inference; running both is a measurement. IF J2 AND J3
;       DISAGREE THAT IS A FINDING, and nothing on this disk records
;       it either way.
;
; THE LAYOUT, WHICH IS THE ONE PIECE OF ARITHMETIC THAT MATTERS.
;
;   From Mesa mesa-24.3.4, src/broadcom/common/v3d_tiling.c, fetched
;   2026-08-27, which is the byte-level definition of every V3D
;   tiling. Two functions, both quoted here because everything below
;   depends on them being read right:
;
;     v3d_utile_width(4) = 4 and v3d_utile_height(4) = 4 (:37-66) - a
;     microtile is always 64 bytes, so at four bytes per texel it is
;     4x4 texels.
;
;     v3d_get_utile_pixel_offset (:70-82), the offset WITHIN a
;     microtile: `x * cpp + y * utile_w * cpp`, i.e. plain raster
;     order inside the tile. Its own comment: "Utiles are 64b blocks
;     of pixels in raster order".
;
;     v3d_get_lt_pixel_offset (:84-102), LINEARTILE:
;       64 * (utile_index_x + utile_index_y) + the offset within.
;     with an assert that one of the two indices is always ZERO -
;     "LINEARTILE is a single line of utiles in either the X or Y
;     direction". Both jobs here are one microtile in one of the two
;     directions, which is exactly the case LINEARTILE describes.
;
;   So, at four bytes per texel:
;
;     lt(x,y) = 64*((x/4) + (y/4)) + (x%4)*4 + (y%4)*16
;
;   For a 4-wide image (x/4 is always 0) that reduces to
;   (y/4)*64 + (y%4)*16 + x*4, which is y*16 + x*4 - the raster
;   offset. Hence J1's expectation. For a 16-wide, 4-high image (y/4
;   is always 0) it is (x/4)*64 + (x%4)*4 + y*16, and raster is
;   y*64 + x*4. Those differ for every texel outside the first
;   microtile. Hence J2's.
;
;   AND LINEARTILE IS THE RIGHT DESTINATION FORMAT FOR THESE SIZES,
;   not a convenient choice: mesa-24.3.4_v3d_resource.c:647-652 picks
;   LINEARTILE for exactly the condition
;   `level_width <= utile_w || level_height <= utile_h`. 4x16 and 16x4
;   both satisfy it.
;
; THE PATTERN, AND WHY IT IS THE BYTE'S OWN OFFSET.
;
;   Input byte at offset k holds the value k. Two hundred and fifty
;   six bytes, two hundred and fifty six distinct values, and a
;   mismatch report can name the SOURCE offset of the byte that turned
;   up in the wrong place instead of just saying the bytes differ. The
;   output page is poisoned with $A5 first, all 4096 bytes of it, so
;   "the unit wrote nothing" and "the unit wrote zeros" are different
;   answers and both are distinguishable from a pass.
;
; THREE THINGS ARE CHECKED BEYOND THE 256 BYTES, all cheap and all
; catching a class of encoding error that would otherwise look
; plausible:
;
;   * THE INPUT IS UNCHANGED. The TFU must not write to its source.
;   * THE OUTPUT PAGE BEYOND THE IMAGE IS STILL $A5. A wrong IOS or a
;     wrong stride produces a correct-looking first tile and a spill
;     past the end, and nothing in the image compare would see it.
;   * THE PAGE TABLE ENTRIES READ BACK AS WRITTEN, so a failure can be
;     attributed to the engine rather than to a table that was never
;     really there.
;
; CACHE COHERENCY - WHICH REGIME THIS RUNS IN, STATED PLAINLY.
;
;   THE ARM MMU IS OFF. This program does not include mmu.pi4 and
;   never calls MmuEnable(), so SCTLR_EL2.M is clear and ARMv8-A
;   treats every data access as Device-nGnRnE. Nothing the CPU writes
;   is ever in a cache and nothing the GPU writes is ever stale, so
;   there is no coherency problem to have. That is deliberate: the
;   question here is "did the engine move these bytes", and running it
;   in the one regime where a cache cannot be the explanation is what
;   makes a mismatch mean what it says.
;
;   THE LIBRARY STILL DOES THE MAINTENANCE. V3dTfuSubmit() takes the
;   byte extent of both buffers and calls V3dCacheRange() over them
;   before the job and over the output after it; with the MMU off
;   those are `dc civac` on lines that were never allocated. They are
;   there so the same code is correct when DRAM is cacheable, and
;   because taking the extents as arguments makes the maintenance
;   structural rather than remembered.
;
;   A GATE CANNOT CHECK ANY OF THAT. a64_v3d_check.py's memory is a
;   flat dictionary of bytes with no caches in it, so it would pass
;   with every cache operation in v3d.pi4 deleted. The cacheable-DRAM
;   run is a BENCH experiment and it is owed; see the report.
;
; THE MEMORY MAP. Everything is inside the low payload window - up to
; $07EFFFFF, its base is above the monitor and `map` prints it - the
; same rule pi4DmaProbe.pi4:127-148 works from, and everything the GPU
; touches is 4 KiB aligned because the
; V3D page table has one entry per 4 KiB and no offset field.
;
;   $400000   this image. --load-addr.
;   $480000   its BSS, 512 KiB above the load address.
;   $640000   THE V3D PAGE TABLE, 16 KiB. Four bytes per 4 KiB of V3D
;             virtual address space, so 4096 entries covering VA
;             $000000..$FFFFFF. Zeroed by V3dMmuInit(), and only the
;             two pages below are ever made valid - every other V3D
;             address in that whole 16 MiB faults by construction.
;   $648000   the MMU's illegal-address page. Where the hardware is
;             told to send an access whose PTE is invalid
;             (MMU_ILLEGAL_ADDR). Nothing reads it; it exists so that
;             a fault has somewhere defined to go, on top of the abort.
;   $650000   the TFU input page, 4 KiB. Image in the first 256 bytes.
;   $651000   the TFU output page, 4 KiB. Poisoned with $A5.
;
;   THE MAPPING IS IDENTITY - V3D virtual $650000 is physical $650000.
;   That is a choice and it is worth naming: it makes every address in
;   the log mean one thing instead of two, and it costs nothing
;   because the table is sparse either way. It does NOT mean the MMU
;   is bypassed - there is no bypass here. Every one of those accesses
;   is a real page table walk, and MMU_HIT / MMU_MISSES are printed
;   afterwards to show that walks happened.
;
; BUILT WITH --entry-returns, WHICH IS WHY THIS IS SAFE TO RUN. It is
; sent to a resident monitor and RETURNS to it, so a wrong answer
; costs a line of output rather than a power cycle:
;
;   pmfc RaspberryPi4\Examples\Diagnostics\pi4V3dTfu.pi4 -t pi4 \
;        --load-addr 0x400000 --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns \
;        -o _work\v3dtfu.img
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN BEFORE RUNNING IT. `deadman 15`. This program starts
; a DMA master with a page table. Every address it can reach is one of
; two pages it mapped itself and everything else faults - but "cannot
; reach" is a claim about this source, and the deadman is a claim
; about nothing at all, which is what makes it worth having.
;
; IT ALSO UNMASKS TWO HUB INTERRUPT BITS, and puts them back. TFUC and
; TFUF, around the jobs only, so that HUB_INT_STS.TFUF - the unit
; reporting a failed conversion - is visible in the log.
;
;   WHY THAT IS SAFE HERE, checked rather than assumed: unmasking a V3D
;   hub interrupt asserts VideoCore IRQ 10 towards the GIC
;   (bcm2711-peripherals.txt:7963 is the one fact the vendor manual
;   carries about V3D). Nothing takes it. This program contains no
;   `enableinterrupts`, and neither does the resident monitor -
;   RaspberryPi4/Monitor/anvil.pi4 has no interrupt enable and no GIC
;   setup anywhere in it, and pmfc's own --entry-returns note says the
;   loader's DAIF is left as it was. An asserted line with no enable
;   and no handler is inert.
;
;   AND IT IS PUT BACK. The mask is restored before this program returns
;   to the monitor, and the mask register is printed BEFORE and AFTER so
;   that claim is checkable in the log rather than asserted in a
;   comment. v3d.pi4 itself never unmasks anything; that is the
;   caller's decision to make and this program is the caller.
;
; THE RETURN VALUE is the number of OUTPUT BYTES VERIFIED, so 768 -
; $300 - on a clean run of all three jobs, and 0 if anything at all
; went wrong. A partial number means some jobs passed and some did
; not, and the log says which.

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mailbox.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/v3d.pi4"

#UART_DR = $FE201000
#UART_FR = $FE201018

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE MEMORY MAP, as constants. See the header.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#TFU_PT       = $640000    ; V3D page table
#TFU_PT_BYTES = 16384      ; 4096 entries, VA 0..$FFFFFF
#TFU_ILLEGAL  = $648000    ; MMU illegal-address page
#TFU_SRC      = $650000    ; input page
#TFU_DST      = $651000    ; output page
#TFU_PAGE     = 4096

; The image. 32 bits per texel, so a microtile is 4x4 texels and 64
; bytes (Mesa mesa-24.3.4 src/broadcom/common/v3d_tiling.c:37-66).
#TFU_CPP        = 4
#TFU_UTILE_W    = 4
#TFU_UTILE_H    = 4
#TFU_UTILE_BYTES = 64
#TFU_IMG_BYTES  = 256      ; 64 texels either way round

; J1 is one microtile wide, J2 and J3 are one microtile high.
#TFU_J1_W = 4
#TFU_J1_H = 16
#TFU_J2_W = 16
#TFU_J2_H = 4

#TFU_POISON_BYTE = $A5     ; the same guard byte memory.pi4:1223 uses
#TFU_POISON_WORD = $A5A5A5A5
#TFU_SRC_FILLER  = $5A5A5A5A ; the input page beyond the image

Procedure PutC(c.i)
  Define f.i
  Repeat
    f = PeekN(#UART_FR)
  Until (f & 32) = 0
  PokeL(#UART_DR, c)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutS(*s)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  Repeat
    ; PeekA, not PeekB. PeekB names the SIGNED .b and sign-extends, so
    ; a byte above 127 would come back negative and this loop would
    ; never see its terminator.
    c = PeekA(*s + i)
    If c = 0 : Break : EndIf
    PutC(c)
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
EndProcedure

Procedure PutNl()
  PutC(13) : PutC(10)
EndProcedure

Procedure PutHex(v.i, digits.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = digits - 1 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      PutC(48 + n)
    Else
      PutC(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure PutDec(v.i)
  Define d.i
  Define started.i
  Define digit.i
  If v < 0
    PutC(45)
    v = 0 - v
  EndIf
  If v = 0
    PutC(48)
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  d = 1000000000000000000
  started = 0
  While d > 0
    digit = (v / d) % 10
    If digit <> 0 Or started = 1
      PutC(48 + digit)
      started = 1
    EndIf
    d = d / 10
  Wend
EndProcedure

Procedure PutPad(*name, width.i)
  Define i.i
  Define c.i
  i = 0
  Repeat
    c = PeekA(*name + i)
    If c = 0 : Break : EndIf
    PutC(c)
    i = i + 1
  ForEver
  While i < width
    PutC(32)
    i = i + 1
  Wend
EndProcedure

; 28 AND NOT 24. The longest label in this program, "bytes written past
; image", is exactly twenty-four characters, so a 24-wide column printed
; its value hard against the text with no space at all - unreadable at
; the bench, and it also broke a gate check that was looking for the
; label followed by a number.
Procedure RowHex(*name, v.i)
  PutS("  ")
  PutPad(*name, 28)
  PutS("$")
  PutHex(v, 8)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure RowDec(*name, v.i)
  PutS("  ")
  PutPad(*name, 28)
  PutDec(v)
  PutNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The error code, spelled out. A number alone sends the reader back
;  to the library to look it up, and one run should be enough.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PutErrName(e.i)
  Select e
    Case #V3D_OK
      PutS("OK")
    Case #V3D_ERR_BRIDGE_LEGACY
      PutS("BRIDGE_LEGACY - $FE00A020 is not 'brdg'. MAPPING.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_BRIDGE_RPIVID
      PutS("BRIDGE_RPIVID - $FEC11020 is not 'brdg'. MAPPING.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_PM_DEAD
      PutS("PM_DEAD - PM_GRAFX read all-ones. PM base is wrong.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_RESET
      PutS("RESET - V3DRSTN did not stick. Password or offset.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_ASB_MASTER
      PutS("ASB_MASTER - master bridge ACK never cleared.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_ASB_SLAVE
      PutS("ASB_SLAVE - slave bridge ACK never cleared.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_IDENT_ZERO
      PutS("IDENT_ZERO - present but dark. Clock or reset.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_IDENT_ONES
      PutS("IDENT_ONES - nothing answers there. Mapping.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_IDENT_VERSION
      PutS("IDENT_VERSION - answering, and not V3D 4.2.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_IDENT_CORES
      PutS("IDENT_CORES - V3D 4.2, but NCORES is not 1.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_NOINIT
      PutS("NOINIT - stage 1 asked for before V3dInit() passed.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_TFU_ABSENT
      PutS("TFU_ABSENT - HUB_IDENT1.WITH_TFU is clear.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_ABSENT
      PutS("MMU_ABSENT - HUB_IDENT2.WITH_MMU is clear.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_ALIGN
      PutS("MMU_ALIGN - an address is not aligned enough.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_RANGE
      PutS("MMU_RANGE - a page is outside the table or is page 0.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_FLUSH
      PutS("MMU_FLUSH - TLB clear or MMUC flush never finished.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_OFF
      PutS("MMU_OFF - the V3D MMU is not on. No page table.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_TFU_ARGS
      PutS("TFU_ARGS - the job description cannot be encoded.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_TFU_BUSY
      PutS("TFU_BUSY - the unit was busy before submission.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_TFU_TIMEOUT
      PutS("TFU_TIMEOUT - CVTCT never moved. The unit did nothing.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_TFU_FAIL
      PutS("TFU_FAIL - HUB_INT_STS.TFUF. The unit said no.")
    Case #V3D_ERR_MMU_FAULT
      PutS("MMU_FAULT - a page table walk failed. See VIO_ADDR.")
    Default
      PutS("unknown code ")
      PutDec(e)
  EndSelect
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  THE LAYOUT MODEL - the only arithmetic in this program that has to
;  be right for the pass criterion to mean anything.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  UtileOffset - the byte offset of a texel WITHIN one 64-byte
;  microtile, at four bytes per texel.
;
;  Mesa mesa-24.3.4 src/broadcom/common/v3d_tiling.c:70-82:
;      return x * cpp + y * utile_w * cpp;
;  with utile_w = 4 and cpp = 4, so x*4 + y*16. Raster order inside
;  the tile, which is what its comment says: "Utiles are 64b blocks of
;  pixels in raster order, with 32bpp being a 4x4 arrangement."
;
;  The two multiplications are written out rather than folded into
;  shifts so that a reader can put this line beside that one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i UtileOffset(x.i, y.i)
  ProcedureReturn (x * #TFU_CPP) + (y * #TFU_UTILE_W * #TFU_CPP)
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  LtOffset - the byte offset of a texel in a LINEARTILE image.
;
;  Mesa mesa-24.3.4 src/broadcom/common/v3d_tiling.c:84-102:
;      return (64 * (utile_index_x + utile_index_y) +
;              v3d_get_utile_pixel_offset(cpp, x & (utile_w - 1),
;                                              y & (utile_h - 1)));
;
;  THE ADDITION OF THE TWO INDICES IS NOT A TYPO AND IS NOT A
;  MULTIPLY. The same file asserts one of them is always zero
;  (:96) - "LINEARTILE is a single line of utiles in either the X or
;  the Y direction" - so the sum is whichever one is non-zero. Both
;  jobs here satisfy that; a caller who broke it would be outside the
;  layout LINEARTILE describes and this procedure would quietly return
;  a wrong answer, which is why the caller checks the shape first.
;
;  MODULO AND DIVIDE, NOT MASK AND SHIFT. The source masks with
;  utile_w - 1 because it knows the width is a power of two. Writing
;  the same thing as %4 and /4 here keeps this line readable next to
;  the quoted one and costs nothing at this size; the values are small
;  and positive so the two are exactly equivalent.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i LtOffset(x.i, y.i)
  Define utileX.i
  Define utileY.i
  utileX = x / #TFU_UTILE_W
  utileY = y / #TFU_UTILE_H
  ProcedureReturn (#TFU_UTILE_BYTES * (utileX + utileY)) + UtileOffset(x % #TFU_UTILE_W, y % #TFU_UTILE_H)
EndProcedure

; The raster offset, for symmetry. Trivial, and written as a procedure
; anyway so the two appear side by side in the source the way they do
; in the comparison.
Procedure.i RasterOffset(x.i, y.i, w.i)
  ProcedureReturn ((y * w) + x) * #TFU_CPP
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  BUFFER SETUP AND VERIFICATION
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  FillSource - the image, then filler over the rest of the page.
;
;  Byte k of the image holds the value k. Two hundred and fifty six
;  distinct bytes, so a byte found in the wrong place names its own
;  source offset. Written a WORD at a time because PokeL is the
;  32-bit store and a byte-at-a-time loop over Device memory with the
;  MMU off is four times the bus traffic for no benefit.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure FillSource()
  Define i.i
  Define w.i
  Define b.i
  i = 0
  While i < #TFU_IMG_BYTES
    b = i
    w = b | ((b + 1) << 8) | ((b + 2) << 16) | ((b + 3) << 24)
    PokeL(#TFU_SRC + i, w)
    i = i + 4
  Wend
  ; The rest of the page, so that a read past the image is visible as
  ; filler rather than as whatever was there before.
  While i < #TFU_PAGE
    PokeL(#TFU_SRC + i, #TFU_SRC_FILLER)
    i = i + 4
  Wend
EndProcedure

; PoisonDest - all 4096 bytes, every time, before every job. Doing it
; per job rather than once is what makes job N's result independent of
; job N-1's: a unit that wrote nothing on the second job would
; otherwise still show the first job's correct output.
Procedure PoisonDest()
  Define i.i
  i = 0
  While i < #TFU_PAGE
    PokeL(#TFU_DST + i, #TFU_POISON_WORD)
    i = i + 4
  Wend
EndProcedure

; Results of the last verification, so the compare can report a
; specific texel rather than a count.
Global badCount.i  = 0
Global badX.i      = -1
Global badY.i      = -1
Global badWant.i   = 0
Global badGot.i    = 0
Global badOffset.i = -1
Global spillCount.i = 0
Global srcChanged.i = 0

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  VerifyImage - every texel of the output against the layout model.
;
;  FULL BYTE COVERAGE, and it is worth stating why rather than
;  assuming it. Sixty-four texels at four bytes each is 256 bytes, and
;  LtOffset is a bijection from those 64 texels onto the 256 byte
;  offsets 0..255 for both of the shapes used here - four whole
;  microtiles, no gaps, no overlap. So comparing texel by texel does
;  compare every output byte, and no byte is checked twice or missed.
;  If the shape changed so that the image did not exactly fill its
;  microtiles, that would stop being true and this comment would be
;  wrong; the shapes are constants at the top of the file for that
;  reason.
;
;  Returns the number of texels that disagreed, and leaves the FIRST
;  disagreement in the globals above.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i VerifyImage(w.i, h.i)
  Define x.i
  Define y.i
  Define want.i
  Define got.i
  Define off.i

  badCount  = 0
  badX      = -1
  badY      = -1
  badWant   = 0
  badGot    = 0
  badOffset = -1

  For y = 0 To h - 1
    For x = 0 To w - 1
      want = PeekN(#TFU_SRC + RasterOffset(x, y, w))
      off  = LtOffset(x, y)
      got  = PeekN(#TFU_DST + off)
      If got <> want
        If badCount = 0
          badX      = x
          badY      = y
          badWant   = want
          badGot    = got
          badOffset = off
        EndIf
        badCount = badCount + 1
      EndIf
    Next
  Next
  ProcedureReturn badCount
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  VerifySpill - the output page beyond the image must still be
;  poison.
;
;  A wrong IOS, a wrong stride or a wrong tiling code can produce a
;  first microtile that looks perfect and a write that runs off the
;  end of the image, and the texel compare above cannot see that at
;  all. Checking the other 3840 bytes costs one loop.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i VerifySpill()
  Define i.i
  spillCount = 0
  i = #TFU_IMG_BYTES
  While i < #TFU_PAGE
    If PeekN(#TFU_DST + i) <> #TFU_POISON_WORD
      spillCount = spillCount + 1
    EndIf
    i = i + 4
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn spillCount
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  VerifySource - the input must be exactly as it was left.
;
;  The TFU reads its source and has no business writing it. A unit
;  that wrote back over its input would still produce a passing image
;  compare for J1, where input and output layouts happen to coincide.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i VerifySource()
  Define i.i
  Define b.i
  Define w.i
  srcChanged = 0
  i = 0
  While i < #TFU_IMG_BYTES
    b = i
    w = b | ((b + 1) << 8) | ((b + 2) << 16) | ((b + 3) << 24)
    If PeekN(#TFU_SRC + i) <> w
      srcChanged = srcChanged + 1
    EndIf
    i = i + 4
  Wend
  ProcedureReturn srcChanged
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  Dump - sixteen bytes a line, for the bench log. Printed on a pass
;  as well as on a failure: a correct dump is what the next person
;  compares against when something changes.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure Dump(base.i, bytes.i)
  Define i.i
  Define j.i
  Define b.i
  i = 0
  While i < bytes
    PutS("    +")
    PutHex(i, 3)
    PutS("  ")
    j = 0
    While j < 16
      b = PeekA(base + i + j)
      PutHex(b, 2)
      PutC(32)
      j = j + 1
    Wend
    PutNl()
    i = i + 16
  Wend
EndProcedure

; ======================================================================
;  ONE JOB, END TO END
; ======================================================================

Global jobBytesVerified.i = 0

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  RunJob - describe it, submit it, wait, and check the bytes.
;
;  Returns the number of output bytes VERIFIED - 256 on a clean job
;  and 0 on anything else. Not a boolean, because the caller adds them
;  up and the total is this program's return value.
;
;  THE ORDER MATTERS. The destination is poisoned BEFORE the job and
;  not after the previous one, so that a unit which did nothing at all
;  leaves poison rather than the last job's answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i RunJob(*name, w.i, h.i, texType.i)
  Define r.i
  Define cs.i

  PutS("-- ")
  PutS(*name)
  PutS(": ")
  PutDec(w)
  PutS(" x ")
  PutDec(h)
  PutS(" texels, RASTER in, LINEARTILE out, texture format ")
  PutDec(texType)
  PutNl()

  PoisonDest()

  r = V3dTfuBegin(w, h, texType)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("   Begin refused: ")
    PutErrName(r)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ; The stride of a tightly packed raster image is its width in
  ; texels - iis = stride_bytes / cpp, and stride_bytes = w * cpp.
  r = V3dTfuSourceRaster(#TFU_SRC, w, #TFU_IMG_BYTES)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("   Source refused: ")
    PutErrName(r)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  r = V3dTfuDest(#TFU_DST, #V3D_TFU_OFMT_LINEARTILE, #TFU_IMG_BYTES)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("   Dest refused: ")
    PutErrName(r)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  PutS("   registers about to be written:")
  PutNl()
  RowHex("TFU_ICFG (plus IOC)", V3dTfuIcfgSent() | #V3D_TFU_ICFG_IOC)
  RowHex("TFU_IIA", V3dTfuIiaSent())
  RowDec("TFU_IIS, texels", V3dTfuIisSent())
  RowHex("TFU_IOA", V3dTfuIoaSent())
  RowHex("TFU_IOS", V3dTfuIosSent())

  cs = V3dTfuCsNow()
  RowHex("TFU_CS before", cs)
  RowDec("  conversions so far", V3dTfuConversionsOf(cs))
  RowDec("  free input slots", V3dTfuFreeSlotsOf(cs))
  RowDec("  busy", V3dTfuBusyOf(cs))

  r = V3dTfuSubmit()
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("   submit refused: ")
    PutErrName(r)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  r = V3dTfuWait(#V3D_TFU_TIMEOUT_US)

  PutS("   after the job:")
  PutNl()
  RowHex("TFU_CS after", V3dTfuCsAfter())
  RowDec("  conversions before", V3dTfuConversionsBefore())
  RowDec("  conversions after", V3dTfuConversionsAfter())
  RowDec("  poll iterations", V3dTfuSpins())
  RowHex("HUB_INT_STS", V3dTfuIntStsAtWait())
  RowHex("MMU_CTL", V3dTfuMmuCtlAtWait())
  RowHex("MMU_VIO_ADDR", V3dTfuVioAddrAtWait())
  RowHex("MMU_VIO_ID", V3dTfuVioIdAtWait())
  RowHex("CORE ERR_STAT", V3dErrStatNow())

  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("   the unit did not report a conversion: ")
    PutErrName(r)
    PutNl()
    PutS("   first 64 output bytes anyway - $A5 throughout means it")
    PutNl()
    PutS("   never wrote, anything else means it wrote and failed:")
    PutNl()
    Dump(#TFU_DST, 64)
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  AND NOW THE ONLY THING THAT COUNTS.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  VerifyImage(w, h)
  VerifySpill()
  VerifySource()

  RowDec("texels wrong", badCount)
  RowDec("bytes written past image", spillCount * 4)
  RowDec("input texels changed", srcChanged)

  If badCount <> 0
    PutS("   FIRST MISMATCH at texel (")
    PutDec(badX)
    PutS(",")
    PutDec(badY)
    PutS("): output offset $")
    PutHex(badOffset, 3)
    PutS(" holds $")
    PutHex(badGot, 8)
    PutS(", expected $")
    PutHex(badWant, 8)
    PutNl()
  EndIf

  PutS("   output, first 64 bytes:")
  PutNl()
  Dump(#TFU_DST, 64)

  If badCount <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If spillCount <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If srcChanged <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  PutS("   VERIFIED: all 256 output bytes are where the layout says.")
  PutNl()
  ProcedureReturn #TFU_IMG_BYTES
EndProcedure


Procedure Main()
  Define r.i
  Define verified.i
  Define j1.i
  Define j2.i
  Define j3.i
  Define maskBefore.i

  PutNl()
  PutS("=== Pi 4 V3D stage 1 - the TFU. Does the GPU EXECUTE? ===")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  Stage 0 first, and it is a precondition and not a formality.
  ;  Nothing below may run against a block that has not identified
  ;  itself: the TFU is a DMA master and V3D 7.1 puts a different
  ;  register at $FEC00400.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  r = V3dInit()
  PutS("0   stage 0, from v3d.pi4: ")
  PutErrName(r)
  PutNl()
  RowHex("HUB_IDENT1", V3dIdent1())
  RowDec("WITH_TFU", (V3dIdent1() >> 17) & 1)
  RowDec("WITH_MMU", (V3dIdent2() >> 8) & 1)
  RowHex("MMU_DEBUG_INFO", V3dMmuDebug())
  RowDec("  MMU version", (V3dMmuDebug() >> 0) & $F)
  RowDec("  VA width bits", (V3dMmuDebug() >> 4) & $F)
  RowDec("  PA width bits", (V3dMmuDebug() >> 8) & $F)
  RowDec("clock 5 MEASURED Hz", V3dClockMeasured())
  PutNl()

  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("STOPPED: ")
    PutErrName(r)
    PutNl()
    PutS("Stage 1 needs a block that has identified itself. Run")
    PutNl()
    PutS("pi4V3dIdent first; its log says which step failed.")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  1a. The V3D MMU. Its own page table, sparse on purpose.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("1a  the V3D MMU - the TFU's addresses are VIRTUAL")
  PutNl()
  PutS("    (v3d_submit.c:1054-1055, 'the TFU is behind the MMU')")
  PutNl()

  r = V3dMmuInit(#TFU_PT, #TFU_PT_BYTES, #TFU_ILLEGAL)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("STOPPED: ")
    PutErrName(r)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  r = V3dMmuMap(#TFU_SRC, #TFU_SRC, #TFU_PAGE)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("STOPPED: ")
    PutErrName(r)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  r = V3dMmuMap(#TFU_DST, #TFU_DST, #TFU_PAGE)
  If r <> #V3D_OK
    PutS("STOPPED: ")
    PutErrName(r)
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  RowHex("page table at", V3dMmuPtBase())
  RowDec("table bytes", #TFU_PT_BYTES)
  RowDec("pages made valid", V3dMmuPagesMapped())
  RowHex("MMU_CTL now", V3dMmuCtlNow())
  RowHex("PTE for the input", V3dMmuEntry(#TFU_SRC))
  RowHex("PTE for the output", V3dMmuEntry(#TFU_DST))
  ; A PTE the CPU wrote and cannot read back is a table the engine
  ; will not find either, and that failure would otherwise show up
  ; only as a page fault with no explanation.
  PutS("    (a PTE is $3")
  PutS("0000000 or-ed with the physical page number, so the")
  PutNl()
  PutS("     two above should read $30000")
  PutHex(#TFU_SRC >> 12, 3)
  PutS(" and $30000")
  PutHex(#TFU_DST >> 12, 3)
  PutS(")")
  PutNl()
  PutNl()

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  The GPU's own caches, once, before the first job. See
  ;  V3dInvalidateCaches()'s header for why once and not per job.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  V3dInvalidateCaches()

  ; TFUC and TFUF visible in HUB_INT_STS while the jobs run. Restored
  ; before this program returns; both values are printed so the claim
  ; is checkable.
  maskBefore = V3dHubIntMaskNow()
  V3dHubIntClear(#V3D_HUB_INT_TFUC | #V3D_HUB_INT_TFUF)
  V3dHubIntUnmask(#V3D_HUB_INT_TFUC | #V3D_HUB_INT_TFUF)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  1b. The three jobs.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("1b  the input image: 256 bytes at $")
  PutHex(#TFU_SRC, 8)
  PutS(", byte k holds k")
  PutNl()
  FillSource()
  Dump(#TFU_SRC, 32)
  PutNl()

  j1 = RunJob("J1", #TFU_J1_W, #TFU_J1_H, #V3D_TEXFMT_R32F)
  PutNl()
  j2 = RunJob("J2", #TFU_J2_W, #TFU_J2_H, #V3D_TEXFMT_R32F)
  PutNl()
  j3 = RunJob("J3", #TFU_J2_W, #TFU_J2_H, #V3D_TEXFMT_RGBA8)
  PutNl()

  ; Put the interrupt mask back exactly as it was found.
  V3dHubIntClear(#V3D_HUB_INT_TFUC | #V3D_HUB_INT_TFUF)
  V3dHubIntMask(#V3D_HUB_INT_TFUC | #V3D_HUB_INT_TFUF)

  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  ;  What the whole run leaves behind.
  ; ------------------------------------------------------------------
  PutS("2   the block afterwards:")
  PutNl()
  RowDec("jobs submitted", V3dTfuJobs())
  RowHex("TFU_CS", V3dTfuCsNow())
  RowHex("HUB_INT_STS", V3dHubIntStsNow())
  RowHex("HUB_INT_MSK before", maskBefore)
  RowHex("HUB_INT_MSK now", V3dHubIntMaskNow())
  RowHex("MMU_CTL", V3dMmuCtlNow())
  RowHex("MMU_VIO_ADDR", V3dMmuVioAddrNow())
  RowDec("MMU hits", V3dMmuHitsNow())
  RowDec("MMU misses", V3dMmuMissesNow())
  RowHex("CORE ERR_STAT", V3dErrStatNow())
  RowHex("CTL_INT_STS", V3dCoreIntStsNow())
  PutNl()

  verified = j1 + j2 + j3
  RowDec("bytes verified, J1", j1)
  RowDec("bytes verified, J2", j2)
  RowDec("bytes verified, J3", j3)
  PutNl()

  If verified <> (3 * #TFU_IMG_BYTES)
    PutS("verdict: TFU_UNVERIFIED")
    PutNl()
    PutS("=== THE GPU DID NOT DEMONSTRABLY EXECUTE ANYTHING ===")
    PutNl()
    PutS("Read J1 first. If J1 passed and J2 did not, the engine ran")
    PutNl()
    PutS("and the tiling encoding or the layout model is wrong. If J1")
    PutNl()
    PutS("failed too, look at MMU_CTL and MMU_VIO_ADDR above before")
    PutNl()
    PutS("anything else.")
    PutNl()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  PutS("verdict: OK")
  PutNl()
  PutS("=== THE GPU EXECUTED. 768 OUTPUT BYTES VERIFIED ON THE CPU ===")
  PutNl()
  PutS("J1 moved bytes. J2 re-arranged them into microtiles. J3 says")
  PutNl()
  PutS("the texture format code did not change the bytes.")
  PutNl()
  ProcedureReturn verified
EndProcedure
